Septate Hyphae
• Almost all species of filamentous fungi possess septate hyphae, indicating their prevalence in these types of fungi;
• In these hyphae, the mycelial body is divided into distinct segments by cross-walls called septa, which serve to compartmentalize the fungus’s structure;
• Septa contain small openings or pores that allow for the passage of organelles and nutrients between adjacent segments, facilitating internal transport within the hyphal network;
• Each segment of the hyphae typically contains one or more nuclei, depending on the specific species of the filamentous fungi, contributing to the overall cellular organization and division of labor among cells;
• When observed under a microscope, septate fungal hyphae often appear as transparent or lightly pigmented, branched filaments that can closely resemble the appearance of certain pathogenic conditions such as aspergillosis, which is a collective term for diseases caused by Aspergilli fungi.

